\pard\sb180\sa160\b\fs31 Extended SRS: From Business Process to API and Data Model\b0\par
\pard\sa100\fs19 Source basis: SRS_TEMPLATE_1.docx (STORAGE, v1.0.0, September 2025), a structured end-to-end SRS illustrated with a food delivery service. This is a new public teaching adaptation. Original embedded images, internal test URLs and old subscriber notices are not reproduced.\b0\par
\pard\sa100\fs19 Unlike the standard SRS template, this source includes business analysis, UI specification, microservice architecture, database design and integration contracts in one delivery package.\b0\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs25 1. Governance and requirements context\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 Original source section  |  Information to capture\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 Glossary  |  Domain entities and technical terms\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 History of changes  |  Version, date, owner and change description\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 Stakeholders  |  RACI responsibilities for analyst, developer, product owner and tester\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 Problem  |  Business pain points, target audience and current process\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 Development goal  |  Value, expected capabilities and business constraints\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 Product description  |  Actors, functions, external dependencies\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 Business processes  |  Process steps and business decisions\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 CJM  |  User journey stages and friction\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 BPMN  |  As-is/to-be flows, actors, gateways and exception paths\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 AS-IS / TO-BE  |  How responsibilities and outcomes change\b0\par
\pard\sa100\fs19 Source example: The food-delivery application aims to improve customer ordering convenience, restaurant reach and order coordination.\b0\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs25 2. Requirements specification\b0\par
\pard\sa100\fs19 The original separates user stories, use cases, functional requirements and non-functional requirements.\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 Field  |  Use\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 Story ID  |  Trace to epic and acceptance criteria\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 Use Case ID  |  Trigger, preconditions, success and alternative flow\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 FR ID  |  Observable system behavior\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 NFR ID  |  Measurable cross-cutting constraint\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 Test ID  |  Verification and test ownership\b0\par
\pard\sa100\fs19 An adapted worksheet should record ID, actor, desired behavior, validation rule, error state and acceptance evidence. The related Food Delivery SRS example includes source-grounded sample functions.\b0\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs25 3. Frontend implementation\b0\par
\pard\sa100\fs19 The original source explicitly includes entry points, design, screen transitions, UI description, user interactions and caching.\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 Screen / interaction  |  Data & action  |  Exception / verification\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 Restaurant listing  |  Search and filter list; select restaurant  |  Empty/loading/error; search pagination\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 Restaurant page  |  View menu and open dish details  |  Dish unavailable or changed\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 Basket  |  Quantity changes and recalculated total  |  Price changed / duplicate tap\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 Checkout  |  Start payment with selected method  |  UNKNOWN outcome and status recovery\b0\par
\pard\sa100\fs19 The last column is additional design-review guidance, not a direct copy of source text.\b0\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs25 4. Backend implementation\b0\par
\pard\sa100\fs19 Original headings include Architecture (C4), Microservices, DB, ER diagram, Description of tables and fields, Database and API mapping, Sequence diagram, API and Message queue.\b0\par
\pard\sa100\fs19 The source includes a conceptual delivery microservice responsible for order, restaurant and support workflows. The public edition makes ownership boundaries explicit; it does not claim they were independently deployed.\b0\par
\pard\sb150\sa100\b\fs22 Component specification table\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 Component  |  Owns  |  Interfaces\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 Merchant Catalog  |  Restaurant and dish read model  |  REST / database\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 Order Service  |  Commercial order, status changes  |  REST / outbox\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 Payment Adapter  |  Processor interaction and status enquiry  |  Provider API\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 Courier Dispatch  |  Delivery assignment and courier state  |  Event / REST\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 Notification Consumer  |  Customer status messages  |  Queue / provider\b0\par
\pard\li180\sa75\fs18 Reconciliation  |  External payment status/financial alignment  |  File/event\b0\par
\pard\sa100\fs19 Source basis: UI and backend section categories, order/courier roles, data-to-API mapping and queue concepts. New additions: distinct payment/reconciliation ownership and resilient integration controls.\b0\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs25 5. Status & role models\b0\par
\pard\sa100\fs19 The source's example includes role permissions for Client, Courier and Technical Support Employee. The status model must reject unsupported direct jumps (for example, New \u8594? Delivered).\b0\par
\pard\sa100\fs19 A new implementation should explicitly define permitted transitions, reason codes, transactional state ownership and role checks.\b0\par
